Use this page to write and run tests for **placing a chicken order in My Chicken App**. Select your platform tab, add the files to the named targets, and run the local tests before preparing cloud execution.

**Start with:** a working app and its approved test dependencies. The examples below provide a small ordering service and its tests. They do not provide a complete screen or backend.

**Work location — your application repository.** Unit-test fakes do not call a real service. Device tests need your actual screen and an approved isolated environment.

## 1. Use these explicit sample rules

These are **teaching assumptions**. Confirm the actual product rules before adapting them for production.

This test fixture adds pricing and availability to the chicken-order scenario. Its types and files are separate from the simpler MVVM demo in Platform & SDKs. Choose the example that matches your feature; do not copy both implementations into the same module or assume their tests are interchangeable.

| Sample rule | Expected result |
| :--- | :--- |
| Item `chicken-grilled`, unit price 1250 minor units | Quantity 2 has a local quoted total of 2500 minor units. |
| Quantity is an integer from 1 to 20 | Reject zero, negative, fractional (JS), or larger values before submission. |
| Item unavailable or metadata invalid | No gateway submission. |
| Order gateway returns `order-001` | Return that actual confirmation, not a fabricated success. |
| Gateway fails or returns an empty order ID | No confirmed-order state. |
| Second tap while one submission is pending | One gateway call through the retained feature instance. |

The local quote is for a single item and excludes tax, delivery, promotions, inventory revalidation, and payment. The real server remains authoritative. This page does not invent HTTP endpoints, payment APIs, or SDK methods.

`requestId` represents a stable identity for one intended order attempt. The actual service must define idempotency and reconciliation. The local in-flight guard does **not** prevent duplicates across restarts, devices, new instances, or unknown-outcome retries. Preserve the request identity and reconcile uncertain submissions before repeating a write.

