## Step 4 — Run the check in the approved Bitrise workflow

Have the template owner place the following **teaching excerpt** in the approved npm workflow after checkout and dependency/tool setup. It is not a replacement for the enterprise template or a complete `bitrise.yml`.

The step creates a fresh report directory, runs the existing coverage script, then verifies that report. `set -euo pipefail` preserves command failures. The required repository status must also be configured to block merging on failure; adding YAML alone does not configure branch protection.

```yaml
# Bitrise excerpt: npm project with approved tooling already prepared.
- script@1:
    title: Execute tests and enforce production coverage
    inputs:
      - content: |-
          #!/usr/bin/env bash
          set -euo pipefail
          mkdir -p .tmp
          MOBILE_COVERAGE_DIR="$(mktemp -d .tmp/js-coverage.XXXXXX)"
          npm run test:coverage -- --coverageDirectory "$MOBILE_COVERAGE_DIR"
          python3 scripts/testing/check-coverage.py \
            --format jest \
            --report "$MOBILE_COVERAGE_DIR/coverage-summary.json" \
            --scope testing/coverage-scope.rn.json
```

A required gate must preserve these distinctions:

| Evidence | Required decision |
| :--- | :--- |
| Tests fail, even with 100% coverage | Block. Assertions still failed. |
| Coverage fails, even with passing tests | Block. The production coverage requirement is unmet. |
| Coverage passes but required device suite is incomplete | Device gate remains blocked/incomplete. |
| Coverage and device checks pass but security/approval is missing | Remaining release gate stays blocked. |

The excerpt measures JavaScript/TypeScript only. It does not configure Android coverage, Xcode coverage, Sauce Labs execution, or NowSecure assessment.

Use [Bitrise Pipelines](/mobile/cicd/bitrise-pipelines/) for approved workflow placement and [Parallel Testing](/mobile/cicd/parallel-testing/) for cross-workflow flag/report handoff. Reports from separate VMs require the confirmed producer/collector contract; local shell variables do not transfer them automatically.

Only use the Python binding provisioned by the approved runner. Native workflows invoke their actual configured test/report/verifier tasks, with the same fail-closed requirement. Configure failure-path evidence collection separately without swallowing the original failure.

## Step 5 — Prove the gate fails correctly

| Deliberate fixture/change | Expected behavior |
| :--- | :--- |
| One production file is not imported by any test | Included with uncovered code; gate fails. |
| Required file missing from report | Gate fails. |
| Missing/corrupt report or empty inventory | Gate fails. |
| Covered count below total | Gate fails. |
| Metric is unsupported | Do not fabricate it; use approved metric mapping and record the limitation. |
| Required suite is skipped or discovers no tests | Required test evidence is incomplete, regardless of percentage. |
| New tracked file under a production root has no report entry | The derived inventory includes it; the gate fails. |
| New production root/target was added | Review and extend scope, report targets, and collection; the helper cannot infer build ownership. |
| Old report copied into this build | Reject through revision/run provenance checks. |

Store source commit, report tool/configuration, production inventory/classification, suite results, metrics, and verifier result with the build evidence. Do not claim a synthetic helper check proves complete native application coverage.


## Inspect and record the result

Open the report's uncovered files/lines, then identify whether the cause is a missing behavior test, incorrect source collection, a missing target, or an ambiguous mapping. Fix the real cause; do not remove the file, weaken thresholds, or fabricate counters.

Record the source commit, build/run reference, tool versions, test counts, scope file revision, required target set, report references, and verifier result. For split native reports, retain every required module's independent outcome. An overall average must not hide a failing module.

